WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.093
"The Best Sleeping Position for
Glymphatic Flow in the Brain"

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.240
Other than getting enough sleep,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.240
what can we do to improve
the glymphatic clearance

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.680
of waste from our brains?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.140
The provision
of a running wheel,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.120
so mice could
voluntarily exercise,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.180
has been shown to improve
glymphatic clearance

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.020
in aging mice,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.360
which was accompanied
by a reduced buildup

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.120
of amyloid deposits
and improved cognition.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.200
Sleeping position
may also make a difference.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.860
Studies on rats show that
their natural sleeping position,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.300
curled up on their sides,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.700
allows for better
glymphatic transport

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.100
than sleeping on their backs
or stomachs.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.740
People also tend
to spend most of their time

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.820
sleeping on their side,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.400
particularly their right side
versus left,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.340
compared to their backs
or stomachs.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.580
This may maximize blood outflow
from the brain.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.560
When we sleep
on our right side,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.020
our right internal jugular vein,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.700
the main blood vessel
in our neck

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.340
draining blood from the head,
is wide open,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.840
and our left jugular

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.540
is partially collapsed
and vice versa.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.740
Since most people have
a dominant right jugular vein,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.740
sleeping on our right side
might maximize brain drainage.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.600
Does it matter?

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.540
Well, people with
neurodegenerative disease,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.660
mostly mild cognitive impairment
and Alzheimer's disease,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.060
tend to sleep more
on their backs

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.800
than those
with normal cognition.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.860
About seventy-two percent
spent at least two hours a night

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.800
on their backs compared
to 37 percent of those

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.000
with healthier brains,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
raising
"the intriguing possibility

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.100
that head position
during sleep

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.640
could influence the clearing
of neurotoxic proteins

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.600
from the brain."

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.980
In crib death,
sudden infant death syndrome,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.620
sleeping position
can be a life-saver,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.320
leading to slogans like
"back to sleep,"

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.160
or more morbidly,
"face up to wake up."

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.760
It's premature
for an adult jingle,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.840
maybe, on your flank
to not draw a blank?

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.080
The characteristic position
of poor sleepers

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.700
is on their back, though,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.220
so maybe it's the poor sleeping

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.260
rather than the position,
per se,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.700
that leads to cognitive decline,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.280
or the causality
could be reversed,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.580
with dementia deteriorating
good sleep habits.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.980
Even if sleeping position
did matter,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.620
it may take a night
in a sleep lab

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.880
to track your movements.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.200
It turns out self-reported
sleep positions are often false.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.860
Should brain benefits to side
sleeping ever be established,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.520
you can train yourself with
so-called "positional therapies"

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.800
such as
the "tennis ball technique,"

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.220
which involves wearing a shirt
to bed backwards,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.040
with a ball stuffed
in the chest pocket.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.240
The uncertainties don't end
with sleeping position.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.360
The glymphatic mechanism itself

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.300
was rapidly embraced
in scientific circles

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.260
and the popular press,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.300
however,
it's been controversial.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.940
It wasn't until 2019

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.680
that the first evidence
was published

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.620
that the glymphatic system
discovered in rodents

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.900
even existed in human brains.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.200
Even the relationship between
sleep and Alzheimer's disease

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.680
is perhaps best summed up
in a recent neurology review

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.200
entitled "It's complicated."

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.260
Yes, those getting
less than 7 hours of sleep

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.940
may have higher rates
of Alzheimer's,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.260
but those getting more than 8
are at higher risk too.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.340
If anything,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.400
population studies show
that longer sleep durations,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.660
more than 8 or 9 hours,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.660
are more strongly linked
to Alzheimer's disease

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.620
and dementia in general

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.200
than sleeping less than
5 or 6 hours.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.400
The association between dementia
and long sleep duration

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.500
could be reverse causation,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.200
where prodromal changes
in the brain

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.120
years before
Alzheimer's is diagnosed

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.560
causes prolonged sleep.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.580
Long sleep duration may also
just be a confounding factor,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.980
a marker of some
underlying health problem

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.540
that's the real culprit.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.080
For example, oversleeping
may be a sign of depression,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.140
which itself is an established
risk factor for dementia.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.580
But there is a plausible
biological mechanism

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.740
for how extended sleep duration

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.320
could increase
dementia risk directly.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.000
Longer sleep duration,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
typically defined as sleeping
more than 8 hours a night,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.240
is associated with signs
of systemic inflammation,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.860
elevated levels of C-reactive
protein and Interleukin 6,

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.440
and both of those
inflammatory markers

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.860
in turn are associated with
an increase in dementia risk.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.260
So much more
needs to be teased out

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.000
about the role
of the glymphatic system

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.640
before we make conscious efforts
to tweak it.

